 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518097 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518097 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518097 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518097 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518097 #edb/ref 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518097 #edb/ref 1089 #edb/ref 17592186518098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518097 #edb/ref 1090 #uuid "a61c5482-22b6-538d-8ab8-e8888d96d94e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518098 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518098 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518098 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518098 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518098 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518098 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518098 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518098 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186518306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518098 #edb/ref 1085 "![](images/75/75f62c88d82d57bb43d69db5b62afd0f990b0a148c5d22cb48b2cfefe7f331aa.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,6].$ It's known that $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ consists of two line segments and a semicircle, as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518098 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "33ad15c6-26fa-53bb-a622-360a616b1dba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518098 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186518278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518103 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518103 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518103 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518103 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the point $(2,\\sqrt{5})$ lies on the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ find ${f}^{″}(2).$\n\n${f}^{″}(2)\\,=\\,$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0343bcd3-e974-592d-9f5b-2241e197ca26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518103 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({f}^{′}(x))\n$$\n\nis the slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$ \n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $x\\in [-3,3]$ is a semicircle of a radius $3$ centered at $(0,0).$ Its equation (for $y>0$) is given by\n\n$$\n(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}={3}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(2,\\,\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518111 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518111 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518111 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131249" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518111 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518111 #edb/ref 1071 "Find all the values of $x$ in the interval $(-6,6)$ where the function $f(x)$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$f(x)$ is strictly increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,,\\,$  \n{{field-2}}\n$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518111 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "168e3b6f-08dd-5743-a200-729abea32e10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518111 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n* If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on $(a,b).$\n\n* If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on $(a,b).$\n\nThe graph below shows that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-3,3).$\n\n![](images/c7/c72ad85a6998493f29289074a4750f2830e8fdefe51d6f81536257abfb8ae008.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly increasing when $x\\in (-3,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518123 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518123 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518123 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518123 #edb/ref 1071 "$f(6)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518123 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35ae1f33-1dce-5ef7-af9a-1cf242b3dd81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518123 #edb/ref 1074 "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $[-3,6]$ consists of two parts: \n\n* an arc of a quarter circle for $x\\in [-3,3],$ which lies above the $x$-axis, and \n\n* a line segment for $x\\in [3,6],$ which lies below the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,3].$ Earlier, we found that\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $f(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6) \\\\  & =2\\pi -6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518134 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518134 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518134 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518134 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $f(3).$\n\n$f(3)\\,=\\,$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518134 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f9b4143-02aa-5852-84a2-99409167bad1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518134 #edb/ref 1074 "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(3)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(3)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(3) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe definite integral of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ over $[-3,3]$ is the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis. The corresponding region is a semicircle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518135 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518135 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518135 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518135 #edb/ref 1071 "${f}^{″}(4)=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518135 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5064a9ef-4f8e-5fbe-8d7d-2164ebe22645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518135 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({f}^{′}(x))^{′}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that $4\\in [3,6],$ where the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is represented by a straight line segment. So, ${f}^{″}(4)$ is just the slope of this segment.\n\nSince this segment passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,0)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(6,-4),$ we can compute its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{-4-0}{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{″}(4)=-\\frac{4}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518136 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518136 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518136 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518136 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518136 #edb/ref 1071 "How many inflection points does the function $f(x)$ have inside the interval $(-6,6),$ and what is the smallest value of $x$ where $f(x)$ has an inflection point?\n\nThere are {{field-1}} \ninflection points in total.\n\nThe inflection point with the smallest $x$-coordinate is at\n\n$x\\,=\\,$ {{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518136 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "51f72acf-5b7c-5f3d-bea9-1b31e5199996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518136 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-6,6].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ Recall the following:\n\n* When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$  \n\n* When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ \n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n* ${f}^{″}(a)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(a)$ is undefined, and \n\n* ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nSo, we summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-6,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,6)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign only at the point $x=0.$  Therefore, $x=0$  is a point of inflection.\n\nFinally, the smallest value of $x$ inside the interval $(-6,6),$ where the function $f(x)$ has an inflection point is $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518168 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518168 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518168 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518168 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518168 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ find the absolute minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,6].$\n\n${x}_{\\text{min}}\\,=\\,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,f({x}_{\\text{min}})\\,=\\,$  \n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518168 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f985c78-b1b4-50b7-b3f8-18827e16fba5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518168 #edb/ref 1074 "The minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\nTo find the minimum value, we apply three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points. From the graph, we see that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and the endpoints of the interval are $x=-6,6.$ So, the candidates are $x=-6,\\,-3,\\,3,\\,6.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Discard any candidates that could not be a minimum.\n\n* The derivative is negative on $(-6,-3),$ so $f(x)$ is decreasing on that interval, and therefore $f(-6)>f(-3).$ So, $x=-6$ cannot be a minimum. \n\n* The derivative is positive on $(-3,3),$ so $f(x)$ is increasing on that interval, and therefore $f(-3)<f(3).$ So, $x=3$ cannot be a minimum. \n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the values of $f(x)$ at the remaining candidates. We have discarded $x=-6,\\,3,$ so the remaining candidates are $x=-3,\\,6.$\n\n* We are given that $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$\n\n* Previously, we found that $f(6)=2\\pi -6.$\n\nSo, comparing $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}≈-7.85$ and $f(6)=2\\pi -6≈0.28,$ we conclude that the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518213 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518213 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518213 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518213 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the value of $x\\in (-6,6)$ where the function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum.\n\nThe relative maximum is at $x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518213 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e8c45a53-973c-5ec6-9492-ec10ad530490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518213 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n* ${f}^{′}(a)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n* ${f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign from positive to negative (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-6,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,6)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=3$ only." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
